{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310578,"nonce":"rh1tc3T3Fg3J6id+v1hRwI+PLVk=","title":"OPEN — `for (v in x)` where `x.iterator()` is DECLARED `:Iterator\u003cT\u003e` emits NO LOOP and silently iterates zero times. Unannotated works. ...","message":"Two-line repro (annotating the return type is the whole trigger):\n```haxe\nclass Holder { public function new() {} public function iterator():Iterator\u003cInt\u003e { return [1,2].iterator(); } }\nclass Main { public static function main() { for (v in new Holder()) Sys.println(\"got=\" + v); } }   // prints NOTHING\n```\nDrop `:Iterator\u003cInt\u003e` and it prints `got=1 got=2`. Same failure with a\nhand-written iterator (`return new It()`), and with `do/while`-style shapes. All\nof these DO work, so the machinery exists: iterating an object with\n`hasNext`/`next` directly, `for (v in [1,2].iterator())`,\n`var it:Iterator\u003cInt\u003e = h.iterator(); for (v in it)`, and `while (it.hasNext())`.\n\n**Where it dies.** `lower_for_in_iterator_protocol` (ir/mir/stmt/for_in.rs)\ncalls `iterator()`, then resolves the ITERATOR CLASS from the method's declared\nRETURN TYPE and looks up compiled `hasNext`/`next` in `class_method_symbols`.\nThe declared `:Iterator\u003cInt\u003e` resolves to the CONCRETE\n`haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator` (verified by printing the symbol name), which is\nruntime-backed and has no compiled methods, so the match falls to `_ =\u003e return`\n— no loop, no diagnostic. `RAYZOR_FORIN_DEBUG=1` shows the path.\n\n**Issue8343's garbage is this bug.** `FAILVALUES 5075949784258078056 != 1`\ndecodes little-endian to ASCII `helloeqF`: with the class path giving up, an\nabstract over `String` falls through to ARRAY-style iteration and reads the\nstring's byte buffer 8 bytes at a time, walking off the end into adjacent heap\n(\"hellogot\", \"=classNa\", \"me:5,fil\"). Note the runtime stores UTF-8\n(`HaxeString { ptr: *mut u8, len }`, no UTF-16 anywhere), so this is not an\nencoding mismatch.\n\n\nInstrumenting EVERY early return (including the inline `_ =\u003e return,` arms the\nfirst sweep missed) pinned it: **`for_in.rs` line ~851**, the `_ =\u003e return` after\n`class_method_symbols` has no `hasNext`/`next` for the resolved iterator class.\n`RAYZOR_FORIN_DEBUG=1` prints `returns_protocol` and the give-up sites.\n\n**The return side is CORRECT.** `Holder.iterator()`'s MIR builds a proper 40-byte\nhandle: `alloc 40`, tag `5934136085303132161` stored at offset 0, obj at 8,\niterator at 16, `fn_ref __vtable_dispatch_thunk__ArrayIterator_hasNext` at 24 and\n`..._next` at 32. So only the CONSUMER is wrong.\n\nRouting that give-up to `lower_for_in_over_iter_handle(pattern, iter_obj, ..)`\n(after splitting the handle loop out of `try_lower_for_in_iter_handle` so a\nregister-based caller exists) DOES emit a correct-looking loop -- tag check, the\nfour loads, `call_indirect` on hasNext/next. But the program then **DIES INSIDE\nTHE LOOP**: `tag_ok=true` prints, `done` after the loop does NOT. At HEAD the\nloop is skipped and `done` prints, so this trade is strictly worse (silent skip\n-\u003e silent death) and was REVERTED.\n\n**THUNK-ABI HYPOTHESIS DISPROVEN.** The WORKING path\n(`var it:Iterator\u003cInt\u003e = [1,2].iterator(); for (v in it)`) stores the SAME\n`__vtable_dispatch_thunk__ArrayIterator_hasNext`/`_next` in slots 24/32, so the\nthunks and the one-argument `call_indirect` are fine.\n\nWhat is left: `var it = h.iterator(); for (v in it)` WORKS, and the only\ndifference in the broken shape is that for-in calls `iterator()` itself and\niterates the RESULT REGISTER with no local in between. Compare the two MIRs for\nwhat the local's assignment does that the direct register use does not (a stack\nslot and reload? a second wrap that is skipped? register liveness across the loop\nblocks?). That difference is the whole bug.\n\n**ATTEMPTS THAT DID NOT WORK (all reverted, 2026-09-06):** routing to the handle\nloop when `iter_protocol_of(return_type)` is Some (it is false — the return type\nis concrete, not the protocol); building a handle from the returned value via\n`iter_source_for` (it yields `IterSource::ClassIterator{ArrayIterator}` and\n`build_iter_handle` then produces something the loop's tag check rejects, so the\nloop runs zero times); and a stdlib-mapping fallback for `hasNext`/`next` in the\n`class_method_symbols` match (never observed to fire — the give-up is EARLIER\nthan that match). Next step: instrument which of the branches in that function\nactually returns, rather than adding more fallbacks to the ones that look right.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): OPEN: `iterator():Iterator\u003cT\u003e` (ANNOTATED) ⇒ for-in emits NO LOOP, zero iterations, silent. Unannotated works. The declared type resolves to the CONCRETE ArrayIterator, which has no compiled hasNext/next ⇒ `_ =\u003e return`. Issue8343's `helloeqF` garbage is the String-abstract fallthrough reading bytes. 3 failed attempts recorded.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_declared_iterator_return_emits_no_loop` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}